<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kandake</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as kandakes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kandake was the title of royal women, often queen mothers or ruling queens, in the kingdom of Kush. Several kandakes held significant political authority and some ruled in their own right. The title, rendered in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Candace, reflects the prominent role of women in governance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kandake.jpg" alt="Kandake" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sven-Steffen Arndt · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aegyptisches_Museum_Berlin_InvNr7261_20080313_Barkenuntersatz_Natakamani_Amanitore_aus_Wad_Ban_Naga_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
